<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null) => (this.get(index) == null || this.get(index).equals(element));</code>

However, the above statement seems to have a logical issue because it suggests that if the element is null, then either the element at the index is null or it equals the inserted element. This doesn't quite match the intended logic. The correct interpretation should be:

- If the index is valid, and the element is null, then the element at the index in the list should be null.
- If the index is valid, and the element is not null, then the element at the index in the list should equal the inserted element.

Thus, the corrected assert statement should be:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => ((element == null) => (this.get(index) == null) && (element != null) => this.get(index).equals(element));</code>

But since the element at the exact index `index` after insertion should be the `element` itself, the more appropriate assert statement would be:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => this.get(index).equals(element);</code>

This ensures that after adding the element at the specified index, the element at that index is indeed the one that was added.
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null || this.get(index).equals(element));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null || this.get(index).equals(element));</code>
